K=[('Circuit selection:','Dominance changes the balance of attacks, retreats and escape types. Frequent subordinate escape does not imply frequent lateral giant activation.'),('Context and history:','LG excitability during interaction differs from excitability at rest. Serotonin’s effect depends on past rank, current experience and drug-delivery conditions.'),('Plasticity over days:','Subordinate serotonin inhibition develops after the initial behavioral decision and can reverse after isolation. Previous dominance can persist in LG modulation after demotion.'),('Synaptic and cellular gain:','Rectifying electrical inputs and membrane conductance contribute to LG responsiveness. cAMP interventions support a postsynaptic facilitatory pathway in the tested preparation.'),('Receptor evidence:','Agonist effects, cloned-receptor signaling and immunostaining answer different questions. The mapped type-1 receptor was not detected on LG; its direct role there is unproven.'),('Behavioral specificity:','Fighting persistence, contest success, walking-leg orientation and post-defeat avoidance are distinct outcomes. Serotonin does not impose a single sign across all cells or behaviors.')]
